Q1. Explain the evolution of programming languages.

Interview Ready Answer

Programming languages evolved to overcome the limitations of previous
languages and make software development easier, faster, and more
maintainable.

1. Machine Language (1GL)
   • Written in binary (0 and 1).
   • Directly executed by the CPU.
   • Very fast but extremely difficult for humans to understand.
   • Machine dependent.

2. Assembly Language (2GL)
   • Uses mnemonic instructions like MOV, ADD, SUB.
   • Converted into Machine Code using an Assembler.
   • Easier than Machine Language but still hardware dependent.

3. Procedural Programming (3GL)
   • Example: C.
   • Organizes code into functions/procedures.
   • Improves readability and modularity.
   • Still difficult to maintain very large applications.

4. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)
   • Examples: Java, C++, C#.
   • Organizes software using Classes and Objects.
   • Provides Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism and Abstraction.
   • Improves code reuse, maintainability, security and scalability.

======================================================================

Q2. What are the major limitations of Procedural Programming?

Interview Ready Answer

Procedural Programming is suitable for small applications but becomes
difficult to manage as software grows.

Major limitations:

• Low code reusability.
• No encapsulation (data and functions are separate).
• Poor data security.
• Tight coupling between functions.
• Difficult maintenance.
• Difficult scalability.
• Difficult team collaboration.
• No inheritance.
• No polymorphism.

These limitations led to the adoption of Object-Oriented Programming.

======================================================================

Q3. If Procedural Programming has many limitations, why is C still
widely used?

Interview Ready Answer

C is still widely used because it provides:

• High execution speed.
• Low memory consumption.
• Direct hardware access.
• Pointer support.
• Predictable performance.
• Minimal runtime overhead.

Common industry usage:

• Linux Kernel
• Embedded Systems
• Device Drivers
• Firmware
• IoT Devices
• Operating Systems

OOP is preferred for large enterprise software, while C is preferred
where performance and hardware control are important.

======================================================================

Q4. What is the difference between Machine Language and Assembly
Language?

Interview Ready Answer

Machine Language

• Binary instructions (0 and 1).
• Executed directly by the CPU.
• No translator required.
• Very difficult for humans to write and debug.

Assembly Language

• Uses mnemonic instructions such as MOV, ADD and SUB.
• Requires an Assembler.
• Easier to read and write.
• Still machine dependent.

Key Difference

Machine Language executes directly.

Assembly Language is first converted into Machine Code by the Assembler.

======================================================================

Q5. Why was Assembly Language replaced by High-Level Languages
like C?

Interview Ready Answer

Assembly Language had several limitations:

• Machine dependent.
• Difficult to write.
• Difficult to debug.
• Difficult to maintain.
• Slow software development.
• Limited language features.

High-Level Languages introduced:

• Functions
• Loops
• Arrays
• Structures
• Standard Libraries

These features improved readability, maintainability, portability and
developer productivity while still providing good performance.

======================================================================

Q6. Can a modern programming language support both Procedural and
Object-Oriented Programming?

Interview Ready Answer

Yes.

Many modern programming languages are multi-paradigm, meaning they
support more than one programming paradigm.

Examples:

C++
• Procedural
• Object-Oriented
• Generic Programming

Python
• Procedural
• Object-Oriented
• Functional Programming

JavaScript
• Procedural
• Object-Oriented
• Functional Programming

Java is primarily an Object-Oriented language, but programs can still
be written in a procedural style using methods and sequential execution.
However, all methods in Java belong to a class.

